[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering ExpectedConditions and Custom Conditions in Selenium"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Explicit waits in Selenium are crucial for handling dynamic web applications. While Selenium provides a rich set of predefined conditions via ExpectedConditions, complex UI scenarios often require custom implementation of the ExpectedCondition interface."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Limitations of Implicit Waits and Thread.sleep"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Using Thread.sleep() introduces unnecessary delays and slows down your test suite. Implicit waits only check for the presence of an element in the DOM, not its interactivity. Explicit waits, combined with ExpectedConditions, allow you to poll the DOM until a specific condition evaluates to true."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Built-in ExpectedConditions"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Selenium provides the ExpectedConditions class which contains numerous pre-built conditions such as elementToBeClickable, presenceOfElementLocated, and visibilityOf. Here is how you apply them using WebDriverWait."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"WebDriverWait wait = new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(10));
WebElement element = wait.until(ExpectedConditions.elementToBeClickable(By.id("submitBtn")));
element.click();"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Why Implement Custom Conditions?"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"There are times when built-in conditions are not enough. You might need to wait for a specific attribute value to change, a localized text to update, or a complex AJAX loading indicator to disappear. This is where implementing the ExpectedCondition interface comes into play."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing a Custom ExpectedCondition"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"To create a custom condition, you implement the ExpectedCondition<T> interface, overriding the apply method. Below is an example that waits until the text of an element matches a specific expected value."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"public class TextToBePresentInElementValue implements ExpectedCondition<Boolean> {
    private final By locator;
    private final String expectedText;

    public TextToBePresentInElementValue(By locator, String expectedText) {
        this.locator = locator;
        this.expectedText = expectedText;
    }

    @Override
    public Boolean apply(WebDriver driver) {
        WebElement element = driver.findElement(locator);
        String currentText = element.getAttribute("value");
        return currentText != null && currentText.equals(expectedText);
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Consuming the Custom Condition in Tests"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once your custom condition class is defined, you can pass an instance of it directly into the WebDriverWait until method, exactly like the built-in conditions."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"WebDriverWait wait = new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(15));
Boolean isTextUpdated = wait.until(new TextToBePresentInElementValue(By.id("statusInput"), "Complete"));

if (isTextUpdated) {
    System.out.println("The status has successfully updated to Complete.");
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Custom Conditions"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep the apply method lightweight because it is executed repeatedly during the polling interval."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Handle StaleElementReferenceException gracefully inside your apply method if the DOM is frequently refreshing."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always ensure your custom condition returns a non-null Boolean or object type depending on your generic type parameter."}]
